From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 08:52:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] crypt32: Implement CryptVerifyTimeStampSignature.

It verifies that an RFC 3161 time stamp token was signed by the
certificate it names, and, given data, that the token is a time stamp of
it; whether the signer is trusted is left to the caller.  What it
returns is as Windows 11 build 29671 has it, measured with
tools/wintrustprobe on Office's and OfficePLUS's time stamps: the context
is one allocation holding the whole token and its TSTInfo decoded, the
signer comes from the token's own certificates and those of the
additional store, wrong data is NTE_BAD_HASH, no data checks no imprint,
and the store and signer may be left out.  CryptRetrieveTimeStamp, which
asks a time stamp authority over the network, is a stub.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

